The Eigenvalue Criterion theorem states that the state y is asymptotically stable if and only if every eigenvalue of the 
jacobian of the system has a negative real part.
This is more easily verified by utilizing the Routh–Hurwitz conditions that state that the eigenvalues of a matrix 
A have negative real part if and only if: 
1. (2×2) tr(A)<0 and det⁡(A)>0;
2. (3×3) tr(A)<0, det(A)<0, and tr(A)a2−det(A)<0, where a2 is the sum of the 2×2 principal minors of A

